In graphical mode, the text layer is displayed in the background, unless a
backdrop picture is displayed instead. All other things displayed in the
graphical mode are slices, which are made using a slice tree, which is
described by this document.

Each pixel of the screen has a colour and a Z-index. The Z-index is a
8-bit number, and when displaying slices on the screen, the Z-index is used
to determine the priority if there is more than one in the same place. The
higher Z-index has higher priority. If the numbers are equal, then the
existing pixel takes precedence if the Z-index is odd, but the new pixel
value takes precedence if the Z-index is even.

The root slice normally takes up the entire 640x350 screen, and will
usually contain other slices, so it should be a type that can contain
other slices, but may be of any type. Some types of slices are able to
contain other slices, as described below.

There are four "levels" of slices, and there can be a root slice in each
level. The levels of slices are as follows:

* Level 0 is used for the main game. This is used for the screen which is
specified by the board info.

* Level 1 is used for item windows and for the special option menu.

* Level 2 is used for text windows, custom windows, and submenus of the
special option menu.

* Level 3 is not rendered, and is used temporarily for holding a slice that
is created at run time.

Each slice has a type, size, optional ID, and any combination of flags:

* NUMBER: Means that this slice has a ID number.

* NOCLIP: Causes the clipping to be ignored for this slice, setting it to
the entire screen (the root slice always does this).

* UNSEEN: The slice is not displayed on the screen (and cannot be used for
graphical collisions). It is still relevant for measuring how much space it
takes up, etc.

* IGNORE: Ignore this slice for most purposes, including measurement,
deciding positioning, etc.

* MANUAL: The metrics are determined manually instead of automatically.

Three more flags are specific to the type of slice, which are called EXTRA1
and EXTRA2 and EXTRA3. Their meanings are described in the below sections.

Some types of slices are "modifier slices". These slices always contain
one other slice, and modifies that slice when it is loaded; the modifier
slice itself does not exist at run time.


=== General controls ===

The bits of the second operand of SLIC have the meanings:

* bit7-bit0 = One of the hexadecimal codes listed below.

* bit8 = Tells whether to use the primary or secondary slice ID. Uses the
primary if this bit is clear or secondary if this bit is set. (This is only
meaningful for direct control by SLIC, and not for SLI0, SLI1, etc.)

* bit9 = Enables chaining. Some slice controls can be used with chaining,
in which case it returns another slice, and then it will use another slice
control on that other slice, with the return value as the input value to
the chained operation. In this case, the "value" parameter (first operand)
has another meaning, which is that only the low 16-bits are the value, and
the high 16-bits will have the meanings that the low 16-bits of the second
operand of SLIC will normally have.

* bit10 = Causes it to work on a root slice of a specified level, instead
of the slice ID number. Where the ID number comes from, instead the low
2-bits will be used as the level to work on. If this bit is set and bit8
is also set, then it will add 2 to the level number.

* bit12 = This bit is used by some controls as an additional input.

* bit13 = Causes it to use zero as the input instead of the specified
value, and the output value is added to the original input value to make
the new output value.

[00] Assign the slice ID to the primary slice ID. If this slice does not
have a ID number, then it is assigned ID 1.

[01] Assign the slice ID to the secondary slice ID. If this slice does not
have a ID number, then it is assigned ID 1.

[02] Recalculate the metrics of slices. This one does not set the size
of the slice which is referenced by this operation, but will affect the
sizes of the slices that it contains (if any).

[03] Recalculate the metrics of slices, like 0x02, but it does set the
size of the slice.

[04] If the slice has the MANUAL flag, then it works like code 0x02 but
the MANUAL flag is temporarily cleared. If the slice does not have the
MANUAL flag then it works like 0x03.

[05] Destroy a slice. This normally only works when specifying the level
and not when specifying the ID, and cannot be used during rendering.

[06] Recalculate the horizontal size; this does not set the size of the
slice but still calls the measurement function, using the input value as
the intended size from its container and the output value will then be
the size which is measured.

[07] Like 0x06 but vertical instead of horizontal.

[20] Read the width.

[21] Read the height.

[22] Read the type of the slice.

[23] Read all of the flags of the slice at once.

[24] Read the ID number of the slice. If it does not have a ID number, then
the value will be unchanged.

[28-2F] Read one of the flags; the result is 1 if the flag is set or 0 if
the flag is not set. The order of the flags is as follows:
  28 NUMBER
  29 NOCLIP
  2A UNSEEN
  2B IGNORE
  2C MANUAL
  2D EXTRA1
  2E EXTRA2
  2F EXTRA3

[30] Set the width.

[31] Set the height.

[33] Set all of the flags of the slice at once, except the NUMBER flag.

[34] Assign a ID number to the slice; only the low 16-bits of the value
will be used. You cannot change the ID number of the root slice, and you
cannot set any other slice to ID number zero; in such a case, nothing will
happen. If another slice has the same ID number, then the slice that had
that number will no longer have a ID number.

[38] If bit12 is set or if this is a root slice of any level, then there
is no effect; otherwise, clears the NUMBER flag and removes the ID number.

[39-3F] If bit12 is set, then sets the corresponding flag; if bit12 is
clear, then clears the corresponding flag. (The flags are the same as those
for controls 0x29 to 0x2F.)


=== ID slice [0] ===

This is a modifier slice which gives a 16-bit ID number to another slice.
ID numbers 0 to 3 have built-in uses:

* 0 = The root slice. (This slice number is automatically assigned and
cannot be reassigned.)

* 1 = A number temporarily assigned to a slice that doesn't have a number.

* 2 = (Reserved)

* 3 = (Reserved)

Other ID numbers can be used for your own use. Slice ID numbers should be
unique (so that two slices on the same screen do not have the same ID
number), but not all slices need to have ID numbers.

The ASN.1 format is two fields, the first being the ID number (which is a
integer) and the second being the slice being modified.


=== Offset slice [1] ===

This slice contains another slice, as well as X and Y offsets (which can be
positive, negative, or zero). The slice is displayed at the specified
offset relative to where it should naturally be displayed.

The ASN.1 format of this slice is: the X offset, the Y offset, and then the
slice that it contains.

Controls specific to this slice are:

[10] Chain to the slice it contains.

[14-15] Forwarded to the slice it contains.

[60] Read the X offset.

[61] Read the Y offset.

[70] Write the X offset.

[71] Write the Y offset.


=== Box slice [2] ===

This slice contains zero or more other slices in order, and displays them
next to each other. You can specify the direction it starts and extends in.

The ASN.1 format of this slice is as follows:

* Enumerated specifying the direction: 0 (top to bottom), 1 (bottom to
top), 2 (left to right), 3 (right to left).

* The border colour and Z-index, which is application type 0.

* The fill colour and Z-index, which is application type 0, and it can
include translucency.

* Boolean which is true to add spacing between items or false otherwise.
It may be omitted, in which case it is false. (This is EXTRA2 flag.)

* The margin, which is a signed 8-bit integer.

* Boolean which is true to clip children or false to not clip children.
(This is EXTRA1 flag.)

* Boolean which is true to try to stretch the non-space nodes so that
they will fit equally, or false to use the normal size of non-space nodes
and then resize the box to fit instead. (This is EXTRA3 flag.)

* A sequence of the slices that it contains.

Controls specific to this slice are:

[10] Chain to a contained slice given a zero-based index number.

[11] Tell how many slices it contains.

[14] Set the border Z-index and fill Z-index, and then call control 0x14
of each slice that it contains.

[15] Relatively set the border Z-index and fill Z-index, and then call
control 0x15 of each slice that it contains.

[44] Read the border colour.

[45] Read the border Z-index.

[46] Read the fill colour.

[47] Read the fill Z-index.

[54] Set the border colour.

[55] Set the border Z-index.

[56] Set the fill colour.

[57] Set the fill Z-index.


=== Space slice [3] ===

This slice is only meaningful inside of a box slice. It is a space with a
variable size, with the direction of the box that contains it. The size
is specified as the natural size, as well as how much it is allowed to
stretch and how much it is allowed to shrink compared with the natural
size. If the total natural size is too small then any stretchable spaces
are stretched to fit, and if too big then shrinkable spaces will shrink.

The ASN.1 format is three integers, being the natural size, the stretch,
and the shrink.


=== Manual slice [5] ===

This is a modifier slice which sets the MANUAL flag of a slice and sets
the manual width and height.

The ASN.1 format is two integers being the width and height, and then the
slice which is being modified. The two numbers may be omitted in which case
the width and height are left unchanged (usually because something else is
setting them instead; if not, then their initial values will be zero).


=== Ignore slice [6] ===

This modifier slice sets the IGNORE flag of the modified slice.


=== Unseen slice [7] ===

This modifier slice sets the UNSEEN flag of the modified slice.


=== No-clip slice [8] ===

This modifier slice sets the NOCLIP flag of the modified slice.


=== Meter slice [9] ===

This slice is used to display a box of a variable size depending on the
value. It can contain up to one other slice, which may optionally be
repeated as many times as will fit. (The inner slice is always clipped.)

The ASN.1 format is:

* Enumerated specifying the direction: 0 (top to bottom), 1 (bottom to
top), 2 (left to right), 3 (right to left).

* The border colour and Z-index, which is application type 0.

* The fill colour and Z-index, which is application type 0, and it can
include translucency.

* The slice it contains, or null if it does not contain any.

* Boolean (the EXTRA1 flag) which is true to repeat or false to not repeat.
This may be omitted (it is false by default) if it doesn't contain a slice.

* The expected size (in the direction that it extends), which is a integer.
If it is null then it will use the size of its container, or the manual
size if it is set.

* A rational number specifying the scale factor of the meter. If this
number is 1/1 then the value will be the number of pixels.

* Specification of the source of the value. This is one of the formats
listed below.

The formats of the source of the value can be:

* Explicit type 0 integer = The literal value directly.

* Explicit type 1 enumerated = A status variable, from 0 (A) to 15 (Z).

Controls specific to this slice are:

[10] Chain to a the contained slice (if any).

[14] Set the border Z-index and fill Z-index, and then call control 0x14
of each slice that it contains.

[15] Relatively set the border Z-index and fill Z-index, and then call
control 0x15 of each slice that it contains.

[44] Read the border colour.

[45] Read the border Z-index.

[46] Read the fill colour.

[47] Read the fill Z-index.

[54] Set the border colour.

[55] Set the border Z-index.

[56] Set the fill colour.

[57] Set the fill Z-index.


=== Text slice [10] ===

Displays text. You can specify the font and colour to use, as well as the
outline/background style, and the spacing.

The possible outline/background styles are:

* None: There is no outline or background. (This is not a separate code,
but is the case when the background colour is set to zero.)

* Solid: Displays a solid background behind each character, with the full
size of the character cell. (This does not include padding, nor is it
possible to use translucent backgrounds, or borders; if you want any of
that then you should put it into a box slice.)

* Outline 1: Displays an outline, around horizontally and vertically
adjacent pixels of the character raster that are set.

* Outline 2: Displays an outline, around horizontally and vertically and
diagonally adjacent pixels of the character raster that are set.

The ASN.1 format is:

* Implicit typed sequence specifying the source of the text to be displayed
according to the list below.

* Horizontal padding (integer).

* Vertical padding (integer).

* Tracking (integer).

* Enumerated specifying the alignment: 0 (left), 1 (centre), 2 (right).
("Left" actually means that the text starts at one end, regardless of the
text direction. For left-to-right, this means it starts at the left, but
other text directions are not implemented yet.)

* Application type 0 specifying the text colour and Z-index.

* Enumerated specifying the font to use, which is 0 for the primary font
and 4 for the built-in PC font (others are not implemented yet).

* Application type 0 specifying the background colour and Z-index.

* Enumerated specifying the background style: 0 (solid), 1 (outline 1),
2 (outline 2).

* Bit string which must have exactly two bits, and controls if it should
avoid measuring the text when measuring the size of the slice. The first
bit is for vertical measurement (the EXTRA3 flag), and the second bit is
for horizontal measurement (the EXTRA2 flag). (This is separate from the
MANUAL flag.)

The sources of the text to be displayed can be:

* Type 0 = Literal text. Contains a PC string, and an optional integer
being the maximum length (if omitted, then it is the same length as the
original text).

* Type 1 = Dynamic string. Contains one integer being the dynamic string
number from 1 to 255, or 0 to display nothing.

* Type 2 = Message line text. Does not have any fields.

* Type 3 = Read from the text screen. There are two fields, both are
integers; the first is the screen position (0 to 1999) and the second is
the maximum length of the text (1 to 80).

* Type 4 = The name of the current board. Does not have any fields.

Controls specific to this slice are:

[14] Set the text Z-index and outline Z-index.

[15] Relatively set the text Z-index and outline Z-index.

[40] Read the dynamic string number or the text screen position.

[41] Read the text into the text buffer. This only works if it contains
literal text and only if the length is not greater than eighty.

[44] Read the text colour.

[45] Read the text Z-index.

[46] Read the outline or background colour.

[47] Read the outline or background Z-index.

[50] Set the dynamic string number or the text screen position.

[51] Copy the text buffer to the slice. This only works if it contains
literal text, and may be truncated to the maximum length.

[54] Set the text colour.

[55] Set the text Z-index.

[56] Set the outline or background colour.

[57] Set the outline or background Z-index.


=== Picture slice [11] ===

...


=== Origin picture slice [12] ===

This slice is like the picture slice but its size for the purpose of of
measurement is always zero, even though the displayed picture might be
bigger than size zero, and that the origin of the picture is moved to the
position of this slice, instead of ignoring it like slice type 11 does.

...


=== Board grid slice [13] ===

This slice displays the contents of one or more layers of the current board
grid, using a loaded picture set.

Do not confuse the board grid slice with the grid slice; they are different
and have different uses than each other.

...


=== Mini map slice [14] ===

...


=== Grid slice [15] ===

Do not confuse the grid slice with the board grid slice; they are different
and have different uses than each other.

...


=== Conditional load slice [16] ===

...


=== Switch slice [17] ===

...


=== Area slice [18] ===

This slice displays its contents at coordinates that are freely specified
for each slice it contains. These coordinates may be relative or absolute.

The ASN.1 format is:

* Boolean (the EXTRA1 flag) which is true to clip the contents.

* Boolean (the EXTRA2 flag) which is true for absolute coordinates or false
for relative to the area slice.

* Sequence of records, each of which is a sequence with three fields: the
X coordinate, the Y coordinate, and the slice.

Controls specific to this slice are:

[10] Chain to a contained slice given a zero-based index number.

[11] Tell how many slices it contains.

[14-15] Forwarded to all of the slices that it contains.


=== Effect slice [19] ===

This slice is used for making special effects on the screen, based on what
is already on the screen, at the same or a different position.

The ASN.1 format is:

* Input coordinates, which is a implicit type sequence, which is type 0 for
the current coordinates, type 1 for relative to the current coordinates,
and type 2 for absolute; type 0 contains nothing but type 1 and 2 contain
two integers being the X and Y offsets.

* Output coordinates, in the same format as the input coordinates.

* Boolean which is true to limit the effect to the width and height of this
slice, or false to use the entire clipping rectangle. This is EXTRA1 flag.
(If this is false and NOCLIP is true, then the entire screen is affected.)

* Octet string specifying the effect parameters (see below).

* Optionally another octet string specifying effect parameters; if it is
present then the effect alternates between them for each pixel.

The format of the effect parameters is:

* Minimum Z-index at the input coordinates to allow the effect.

* One more than maximum Z-index at input coordinates to allow the effect.

* Minimum Z-index at the output coordinates to allow the effect.

* One more than maximum Z-index at output coordinates to allow the effect.

* Specifies how the new Z-index is relative to: 0 means absolute, 1 means
add the value at the input coordinates, 2 means add the value at the output
coordinates, 3 means add both. These are added to the next byte value and
then modulo 256.

* The Z-index adjustment value.

* The AND mask for the colour at the input coordinates.

* The AND mask for the colour at the output coordinates.

* The XOR mask for the colour.

Controls specific to this slice are:

[60] Read the output X offset.

[61] Read the output Y offset.

[62] Read the input X offset.

[63] Read the input Y offset.

[70] Write the output X offset.

[71] Write the output Y offset.

[72] Write the input X offset.

[73] Write the input Y offset.

The EXTRA2 and EXTRA3 flags will be set if the input coordinates and if
the output coordinates are relative.


=== Layered slice [20] ===

This slice contains several other slices, which are all displayed at the
same place (rather than a box slice which places them next to each other).

The ASN.1 format is:

* Boolean (EXTRA1 flag) which is true to clip the contents.

* Sequence of the slices that it contains.

Controls specific to this slice are:

[10] Chain to a contained slice given a zero-based index number.

[11] Tell how many slices it contains.

[14-15] Forwarded to all of the slices that it contains.


=== Isolation slice [21] ===

This slice contains one other slice, and it will isolate some of the
functions of slices from outer slices.

You can enable or disable measuring inner slices. If enabled, then although
it will be measured, the result is ignored.

The ASN.1 format is:

* The slice that it contains.

* Two integers being the width and height of the inner slice. You can
optionally omit both, but cannot omit only one of them.

* Optional boolean, which is true to disable measuring the inner slice
when the outer slice is measured, or false to enable measuring. The default
value is true. This is the EXTRA1 flag.

Controls specific to this slice are:

[10] Chain to the slice it contains.

(Controls 0x14 and 0x15 are ignored; they are not forwarded.)

